<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer avoided a parliamentary ethics inquiry on Tuesday after MPs voted 335 to 223 against a Conservative motion to refer him to the Commons Privileges Committee over his statements about appointing Peter Mandelson as ambassador to the United States. The proposed inquiry would have examined whether Starmer misled the House of Commons by saying &quot;full due process&quot; had been followed in the appointment. Starmer appointed Mandelson in December 2024 and dismissed him in September 2025 after Mandelson’s ties to the late U.S. sex offender Jeffrey Epstein were found to have been deeper than previously known. Starmer has said Mandelson lied about his relationship with Epstein and that officials kept information from him about vetting that would have stopped him from making the appointment. Reports cited in the debate said UK Security Vetting had recommended Mandelson not be granted clearance before Foreign Office officials approved him without informing the prime minister. The vote followed evidence from officials and former aides about the appointment process, including testimony from former chief of staff Morgan McSweeney, who apologized for recommending Mandelson but denied pressing officials to bypass vetting steps. Labour MPs were ordered to oppose the motion, while 15 Labour backbenchers rebelled and 53 did not vote. Starmer faces Prime Minister’s Questions on Wednesday, the last before Parliament is prorogued ahead of a new session on May 13.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/M3nPq9hlSd0Wtvx7J07vEeZ8aEj0H-TpcVEV9MG484qj7skr-Qy6183rIVtC_rsCTCoe_tpNO80NnvdLfUgHGMP8MF_TMMUdlA0JniwEcnGA' alt='Britain&#x27;s Prime Minister Keir Starmer holds a press conference at the end of his cabinet&#x27;s first meeting in Downing Street in London, UK on July 6.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Speaker approval: Commons Speaker Lindsay Hoyle approved Kemi Badenoch’s request for MPs to debate and vote on a possible Privileges Committee referral.</li><li>Debate fallout: During the six-hour Commons debate, some Labour MPs warned that blocking a referral risked looking like a cover-up.</li><li>Vetting sequence: Former Foreign Office chief Sir Philip Barton told MPs the appointment was announced before security vetting had been completed, which he described as unusual.</li><li>Robbins dispute: Starmer sacked Sir Olly Robbins for not telling him about UKSV findings; Robbins later alleged there had been &quot;constant pressure&quot; from No. 10 over approving developed vetting.</li><li>Backbench reaction: Some Labour backbenchers argued Starmer should refer himself to the Privileges Committee to clear his name.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Kemi Badenoch and the Conservative Party: Badenoch accused Starmer of misleading the Commons over claims that &quot;full due process&quot; had been followed and that no pressure was exerted in Mandelson’s appointment. (<a href='https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/mps-reject-bid-to-investigate-claim-pm-misled-commons-over-mandelson-appointment-Z5V2YL5UTNJNZGITVB2UELV2PQ/'>Irish News</a>)</li><li>Downing Street: A spokesperson for Starmer’s office described the push for a vote as a &quot;desperate political stunt&quot; ahead of local elections. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995668/uk-pm-may-face-probe-for-misleading-parliament-on-mandelson'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Morgan McSweeney: Starmer’s former chief of staff said Mandelson’s eventual disclosures about Epstein were &quot;way, way, way worse&quot; than understood, and rejected an allegation that he had sworn at a Foreign Office boss during vetting discussions. (<a href='https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/uk/2026/04/28/keir-starmer-sacked-chief-of-staff-backs-pm-in-mandelson-security-vetting-affair/'>The National News</a>, <a href='https://www.standard.co.uk/news/politics/keir-starmer-foreign-office-prime-minister-jeffrey-epstein-b1280283.html'>Evening Standard</a>)</li><li>Sir Philip Barton: The former Foreign Office permanent under-secretary said there had been an &quot;uninterested&quot; attitude toward securing Mandelson’s high-level clearance and suggested there was pressure to &quot;get on with&quot; approving developed vetting. (<a href='https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/starmers-due-process-claim-over-mandelson-job-not-backed-by-ex-official-ZZUO5RCOWFJS3MSA26FJWKV7PQ/'>Irish News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://novaramedia.com/2026/04/28/starmer-on-the-ropes-over-questions-of-dishonesty/'>Starmer on the Ropes Over Questions of Dishonesty</a> - novaramedia.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/starmer-avoids-ethics-probe-over-mandelson-appointment-but-challenges-lie-ahead'>Starmer avoids ethics probe over Mandelson appointment but challenges lie ahead</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/04/28/uk-pm-starmer-avoids-parliamentary-probe-over-mandelson-appointment-as-us-envoy'>UK PM Starmer avoids parliamentary probe over Mandelson appointment as US envoy</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3351759/uk-pm-starmer-avoids-parliament-probe-over-epstein-associate-peter-mandelson'>UK PM Starmer avoids parliament probe over Epstein associate Peter Mandelson</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995876/keir-starmer-escapes-probe-on-misleading-parliament'>Keir Starmer escapes probe on ‘misleading parliament’</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/uk-pm-keir-starmer-wins-crunch-parliament-vote-over-ethics-inquiry/article70918043.ece'>U.K. PM Keir Starmer wins crunch Parliament vote over ethics inquiry</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.heraldscotland.com/news/26061714.mps-vote-sleaze-probe-keir-starmer-mandelson-vetting/'>Keir Starmer avoids sleaze probe over Mandelson vetting as MPs vote against inquiry</a> - heraldscotland.com</li><li><a href='https://www.24heures.ch/affaire-mandelson-epstein-k